/**
\defgroup filesystem Filesystem
\brief Finds every file under `Resources/`, gives each one an asset path, and loads files into typed asset objects on demand.

<table class="markdownTable">
<tr><td><b>Source</b></td><td>\srcdir{include/Filesystem} · \srcdir{src/Filesystem} · \src{Config/filesystem.json}</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Include</b></td><td>`#include <Filesystem/Module.hpp>` (registry) · `#include <Filesystem/Importers/Importer.hpp>` (loading)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Namespaces</b></td><td>`Filesystem`, `Filesystem::Importers`; the base asset types `SAsset`, `STextFileAsset` and `SRawFileAsset` are global</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Started by</b></td><td>\srclink{src/Module.cpp,84,Engine::InitializePlugins()} calls Filesystem::InitializeAllResources(), second after Log</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Example</b></td><td>\ref ex_assets</td></tr>
</table>

## How to use

1. **Put files under `Resources/`** in your game folder. After a build they are copied next to the executable, and at startup every file is registered.
2. **Refer to a file by its asset path**: its path under `Resources/`, without the extension, with the platform's separators. `Resources/Images/Player.png` is `Images\Player`, written `"Images\\Player"` in C++.
3. **Look it up** with Filesystem::GetAssetData() to get its Filesystem::SAssetData: the real file path, name, extension and type.
4. **Load it** with `Filesystem::Importers::LoadFile<T>(path)`. `T` is the asset type you want: `STextFileAsset`, `SRawFileAsset`, `Graphics::STexture2D`, or your own. The result is cached, so loading again is cheap.
5. **Store references** in components as a Filesystem::SSoftAssetPath (just the path string), not as loaded objects.

\genexopen{modules/AssetsAndMemory.cpp,Listing assets and reading one}
\snippet modules/AssetsAndMemory.cpp listing
\snippet modules/AssetsAndMemory.cpp asset_data
\endex

\genexopen{modules/AssetsAndMemory.cpp,Loading a text file}
\snippet modules/AssetsAndMemory.cpp load_text
\endex

Sprites use asset paths directly. The renderer loads and uploads the texture the first time it draws it:

```cpp
.set<Components::FTexture2DComponent>({
    .Sprite = { .SpriteSheet = {"Images\\Player"} },
    .TextureSize = {32, 32},
})
```

## Asset paths {#filesystem_paths}

| File on disk | Asset path | Asset name |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `Resources/CollisionTexture.png` | `CollisionTexture` | `CollisionTexture` |
| `Resources/Images/Player.png` | `Images\Player` | `Player` |
| `Resources/Shaders/Compiled/PullSpriteBatch.vert.spv` | `Shaders\Compiled\PullSpriteBatch.vert` | `PullSpriteBatch.vert` |
| `Resources/Config/filesystem.json` | `Config\filesystem` | `filesystem` |

The asset path is computed by Filesystem::FilePathToAssetPath() (\src{src/Filesystem/FilesystemTypes.cpp,49,53}): the path relative to `Resources/`, with the last extension removed.

- Filesystem::GetAssetData() converts `/` to `\` before looking up (\src{src/Filesystem/Module.cpp,67,76}), so `"Images/Player"` works there.
- Filesystem::IsValid() does **not** convert (\src{src/Filesystem/Module.cpp,119,129}), and the renderer uses it to decide whether to draw a sprite. **Always use `\\` in paths you store in components.** For paths built at runtime, `std::filesystem::path(p).make_preferred().string()` gives the right form.
- Two files that differ only in their extension get the same asset path. Only the first one found is registered. That is why the three compiled forms of each shader (`.dxil`, `.msl`, `.spv`) share one asset: the renderer picks the right file itself.

## Asset types

`Resources/Config/filesystem.json` maps file extensions to Filesystem::EAssetType values. Each file's type is set when it is registered (\src{src/Filesystem/FilesystemTypes.cpp,24,43}):

\engex{Config/filesystem.json,1,9,The engine's extension table}
```cpp
{
  "AssetTypes" : {
    "TEXTURE": [".png", ".jpg"],
    "SHADER": [".dxil", ".spv", ".msl"],
    "FONT": [".ttf", ".otf"],
    "PREFAB": [".prefab"],
    "LEVEL": [".level"]
  }
}
```
\endex

The type is only a label. `LoadFile<T>` decides how to read a file from `T`, not from the asset type. The type is used by Filesystem::GetAllAssetPaths(EAssetType) and by editor drop-downs that list, for example, only textures.

\note The engine copies its own `Config/` folder to `<output>/Config/Resources`, not to `<output>/Resources/Config` (\src{cmake_modules/MoveConfigFiles.cmake,9,16}). Copy `filesystem.json` into your game's `Resources/Config/` yourself, as described in \ref first_game.

## Loading and caching

`LoadFile<T>(path)` finds the asset's Filesystem::SAssetData and returns `SAssetData::ObjectData` if it is already loaded. Otherwise it calls the loader for `T`, stores the result in `ObjectData`, sets the asset's `AssetPath` and name, and returns it (\src{include/Filesystem/Importers/Importer.hpp,63,76}).

| Loader for | Reads the file as | Defined in |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `STextFileAsset` | Text, in `Data` (`std::string`) | \src{src/Filesystem/Importers/Importer.cpp,40,50} |
| `SRawFileAsset` | Bytes, in `Data` (`std::vector<uint8_t>`) | \src{src/Filesystem/Importers/Importer.cpp,27,38} |
| `Graphics::STexture2D` | An image (SDL_image), converted to RGBA | \src{src/Types/Graphics.cpp,51,73} |
| `Graphics::SSpriteSheet` | Same as a texture | \src{src/Types/Graphics.cpp,75,98} |
| LDtk project and level types | JSON, via nlohmann | \src{src/Filesystem/Importers/Importer.cpp,9,25} |

\warning An asset is cached once, for the first type it is loaded as. Loading the same path again as a *different* type returns the cached object cast to the new type, which is wrong. Load each file as one type only.

### Your own asset types

Derive from `SAsset`, then specialize Filesystem::Importers::LoadAsset for your type. #DEFINE_ASSET_LOADER declares the specialization:

