\snippet modules/AssetsAndMemory.cpp custom_asset
\snippet modules/AssetsAndMemory.cpp load_custom
\endex

### Assets made in code

Register a Filesystem::SAssetData whose `ObjectData` is already set. `LoadFile` then returns that object without touching the disk, and the renderer can draw it like any image. This is how every example creates its textures:

\genexopen{modules/AssetsAndMemory.cpp,Registering a texture generated at runtime}
\snippet modules/AssetsAndMemory.cpp generated_texture
\snippet modules/AssetsAndMemory.cpp register_runtime
\endex

Assets can be registered before `Engine::InitializePlugins()` (needed for `CollisionTexture`) or at any time after. A file found by the startup scan with the same asset path is skipped.

## Config files

Filesystem::SConfig is a small helper for JSON settings files under `Resources/Config/`. A config type derives from `SConfig<Self>`, says where its file is, and lists its fields for nlohmann/json. The engine's own Filesystem::SFilesystemConfig reads `filesystem.json`:

\engex{include/Filesystem/Config.hpp,57,65,SFilesystemConfig}
```cpp
struct SFilesystemConfig : SConfig<SFilesystemConfig> {
    std::map<std::string, std::vector<std::string>> AssetTypes;

    std::filesystem::path GetConfigPath() override {
        return std::filesystem::path("Config") / "filesystem";
    }

    NLOHMANN_DEFINE_TYPE_INTRUSIVE(SFilesystemConfig, AssetTypes)
};
```
\endex

\engex{src/Filesystem/FilesystemTypes.cpp,29,31,Reading it}
```cpp
TObjectPtr<SFilesystemConfig> config = SFilesystemConfig::Get();

for (const auto& assetType: config->AssetTypes) {
```
\endex

Only `Get()` works at this commit: it parses the file on every call. `Read()` and `Save()` fail to compile if you call them. See \ref known_issues_filesystem.

## Things to watch out for

- `Resources/Config` must exist in the working directory, or Filesystem::InitializeAllResources() throws (\src{src/Filesystem/Module.cpp,27,29}).
- At startup the log shows `Could not find asset [Config\filesystem]` once, even when the file is there: the file's own type is looked up before it is registered. It is harmless.
- `LoadFile(path)` throws `AssetCouldNotLoadException` when the path is unknown. `TryLoadFile(path)` throws `nullptr` instead; it does not return an empty pointer. Check with `AssetExists<T>(path)` or Filesystem::IsValid() first.
- Filesystem::Module() is declared but not defined. Don't call it; the engine calls Filesystem::InitializeAllResources().
- More in \ref known_issues_filesystem.
*/

/**
\fn void Filesystem::InitializeAllResources()
\ingroup filesystem
\brief Scans `Resources/Config`, then all of `Resources/`, and registers every file as an asset. Called by Engine::InitializePlugins().

Files are not read yet: only an Filesystem::SAssetData is created for each. A file whose asset path is already registered is skipped. Defined at \src{src/Filesystem/Module.cpp,24,50}.
*/

/**
\fn void Filesystem::RegisterAssetData(const TObjectPtr<SAssetData> &AssetData)
\ingroup filesystem
\brief Adds an asset to the registry under `AssetData->AssetPath`. Use it for assets made in code.

If `AssetData->ObjectData` is set, `LoadFile` returns that object instead of reading a file. Registering a path that already exists keeps the existing entry. Defined at \src{src/Filesystem/Module.cpp,52,65}.

\genexopen{modules/AssetsAndMemory.cpp,Generated texture}
\snippet modules/AssetsAndMemory.cpp generated_texture
\endex
*/

/**
\fn TObjectPtr<SAssetData> Filesystem::GetAssetData(const std::string& AssetPath)
\ingroup filesystem
\brief Returns the registered asset with this asset path, or an empty pointer (and an error in the log) if there is none.

Accepts `/` or `\` separators. Defined at \src{src/Filesystem/Module.cpp,67,76}.
*/

/**
\fn TObjectPtr<SAssetData> Filesystem::GetAssetData(const TObjectPtr<SAsset>& Asset)
\ingroup filesystem
\brief Returns the registered asset for a loaded asset object.

Looks the asset up by its *name* (`Asset->GetName()`), not its path (\src{src/Filesystem/Module.cpp,78,81}), so it only finds assets stored directly under `Resources/`. Prefer `Asset->GetAssetData()`, which uses the path.
*/

/**
\fn std::vector<std::string> Filesystem::GetAllAssetPaths(const std::string &RelativePath)
\ingroup filesystem
\brief Returns the asset paths of every registered asset, or of those inside the folder `RelativePath` (for example `"Images"`).

Defined at \src{src/Filesystem/Module.cpp,83,105}.
*/

/**
\fn std::vector<SSoftAssetPath> Filesystem::GetAllAssetPaths(EAssetType AssetType)
\ingroup filesystem
\brief Returns every registered asset of one type, for example all `Filesystem::TEXTURE` assets.

Defined at \src{src/Filesystem/Module.cpp,107,109}.
*/

/**
\fn std::filesystem::path Filesystem::GetResourcesDirectory()
\ingroup filesystem
\brief `<current working directory>/Resources`. Defined at \src{src/Filesystem/Module.cpp,111,113}.
*/

/**
\fn std::filesystem::path Filesystem::GetConfigDirectory()
\ingroup filesystem
\brief `<current working directory>/Resources/Config`. Defined at \src{src/Filesystem/Module.cpp,115,117}.
*/

/**
\fn bool Filesystem::IsValid(const SSoftAssetPath& Path)
\ingroup filesystem
\brief Returns `true` if `Path` is a registered asset path. The comparison is exact: use the platform's separators.

Defined at \src{src/Filesystem/Module.cpp,119,129}. The renderer skips sprites whose texture path is not valid.
*/

